  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>correct approach is to update the CloudFormation template to have the Auto Scaling group refer to the earlier Launch Template version</b>. This makes sure that your infrastructure is managed through CloudFormation and remains in the desired state. Manually deleting template versions (like in <i>\"Delete the latest Launch Template version using the AWS CLI command...\"</i>) is risky, since it may leave your stack out of sync with its template, leading to further drift or errors. Modifying resources directly in the EC2 console (as in <i>\"In the EC2 web console, set your Auto Scaling group to use the default Launch Template version...\"</i>) also results in stack drift, since CloudFormation will not recognize manual changes and may overwrite them during subsequent updates. Simply changing the AMI ID in the Launch Template resource in CloudFormation (<i>\"Update the CloudFormation template to change the Launch Template's AMI ID field back...\"</i>) creates a brand new version—so your ASG, still set to use the latest version, wouldn’t revert to the old, known-good configuration. Best practices dictate managing version references to ensure reproducibility and auditability within CloudFormation. Whenever rolling back a resource change, it's safest to explicitly specify the version or configuration known to be stable and let CloudFormation handle all changes for traceability. This approach minimizes risk and maintains a clean infrastructure-as-code workflow.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are managing a CloudFormation stack that provisions both a Launch Template and an Auto Scaling group, where the Auto Scaling group references the most recent version of the Launch Template. Recently, after updating the CloudFormation template to point the Launch Template to a new AMI ID, you discover new Auto Scaling group instances are failing to start web applications correctly due to a problem with the new AMI. You need to reverse these changes and ensure the group uses the previous, functioning AMI configuration. What is the best solution?</p>",
